    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/09/hoka-mach-x-3-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The high-stack dual-density setup (soft, bouncy PEBA top layer over a firmer base) rides firmer overall, with better forefoot feel/pop than heel comfort and can feel jarring on steep downhills.",
        "Responsiveness: Energy return is a standout, driven by a stiff Pebax plate and aggressive rocker that create bounce, snappy toe-off, and smooth transitions at steady-to-uptempo paces.",
        "Stability: It is generally stable for a tall shoe thanks to sidewalls/geometry and feels confident in turns, but rearfoot stability is less secure and can feel clunky for heel-oriented runners; heel counter is flexible but hold is mostly secure.",
        "Durability: Outsole wear appears solid so far with good traction (including wet roads) and no major slippage, while overall durability is characterized as average."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/09/hoka-mach-x-3-multi-tester-review-3.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: High stack (W 42/38 mm, M 44/39 mm) and dual-density PEBA deliver comfortable impact protection, with a soft-smooth feel that is more dynamic/firm than mushy through heel and forefoot.",
        "Responsiveness: The PEBA foam plus PEBAX plate is repeatedly described as propulsive and energetic, with clear pop at faster paces, though the very rigid setup feels more rebound-focused than snappy rocker roll-through.",
        "Stability: The platform is very rigid and runs more controlled than wild; testers reported good lateral support and reduced ankle-roll risk, and the updated padded/flared heel counter improves rearfoot security (with minor initial heel slippage for one tester).",
        "Durability: Long-term durability evidence is limited in this review; testers mention good outsole performance and improved upper/heel-collar construction, but do not provide extended outsole-wear or material-longevity results."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/hoka-mach-x-3-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The dual-layer midsole has a soft PEBA top for plush initial contact and impact absorption, while the EVA/plate underlayer makes heel-to-forefoot feel firmer and more speed-oriented than max-plush; some testers said it now feels flat versus newer rivals.",
        "Responsiveness: The Pebax plate and firmer EVA deliver a snappy toe-off with noticeable pep, but reviewers also reported reduced bounce/pop compared with current super-trainer options, partly due to unchanged foam tuning and added weight.",
        "Stability: Upper and heel updates add structure (more traditional heel, padded collar/tongue), and the Warp Knit fit is secure/locked-in; the plate feels responsive without being overly rigid, supporting controlled transitions.",
        "Durability: Outsole performance was a standout, with strong wet/dry traction and little to no visible wear during testing; multiple reviewers expected the outsole/build to last well over time."
      ]
    }
  ],
